R7a — the play-space split, the viewmodel mount, the VR crosshair gate, and the HUD extents.

Four changes, VR-gated, all no-ops when q2vr.active is 0 (so the macOS oracle and every
flat build are byte-identical). Each carries its own mechanism comment at the site.

  * shared.h  — bodyofs/body_yaw/baseorg appended to the q2vr block.
  * view.c    — the head's PLAY-SPACE displacement takes a yaw-only basis; only the eye's
                head-local offset takes the finished view angles. This is the look-jitter
                root cause (Q-VR8 item 2).
  * entities.c— the viewmodel takes the same yaw-only play basis, so the gun stops swinging
                around the player on every head turn.
  * screen.c  — SCR_HudScaleChanged also re-takes the HUD EXTENTS from r_config (the wheel
                clipping, Q-VR8 item 5), and the flat crosshair is gated off when the VR
                world-space aim dot is the crosshair (item 3).


--- a/inc/shared/shared.h
+++ b/inc/shared/shared.h
@@ -1373,6 +1373,39 @@
     int     vm_mounted;         // WRITTEN BY THE ENGINE: 1 = mounted to the hand this frame
     float   dot_range;          // WRITTEN BY THE ENGINE: how far the aim trace reached
     int     dot_drawn;          // WRITTEN BY THE ENGINE: 1 = the dot was actually added
+
+    // [vr overlay R7a] THE PLAY-SPACE SPLIT. APPENDED, never inserted, for the fourth time.
+    //
+    // THE BUG THIS EXISTS TO END (Q-VR8 item 2, "the images jitter in both eyes as I look
+    // left and right, slightly shaking in place"). Until now the head's own displacement rode
+    // inside `eyeofs`, and the comment above it — "head-local axes" — was true of the eye's
+    // half and FALSE of the head's. The head term comes from `baseFromWorld * deviceAnchor`,
+    // i.e. the head's position in PLAY SPACE, whose axes are fixed to the recentre yaw and do
+    // not turn when the head turns. Rotating it by the finished view angles therefore swung
+    // the camera around the play origin by the head's own yaw, pitch and roll: turn your head
+    // 30 degrees with the head 15 cm off the recentre point and the camera translates ~8 cm,
+    // in both eyes, only while the head moves. That is the jitter, and it is first-order —
+    // the compositor then reprojects pixels that do not correspond to the anchor it was
+    // given, which is why depth (`q2vrdepthfloor`) modulates its character without being its
+    // cause.
+    //
+    // The split is the fix, and it is a split of BASES, not of magnitudes:
+    //   * `eyeofs`  — this eye's offset from the head, genuinely head-local, rotated by the
+    //                 finished view angles. Unchanged in meaning; it now carries ONLY the eye.
+    //   * `bodyofs` — the head (and the hand) in play space, rotated by a YAW-ONLY basis built
+    //                 from `body_yaw` + `server_yaw`. No pitch, no roll, no head yaw: leaning
+    //                 your head does not rotate the room.
+    // `body_yaw` is the shell's own body yaw — what the stick and the recentre own — which is
+    // exactly `viewangles[YAW]` MINUS the head yaw, and is passed rather than re-derived
+    // because subtracting one angle from another to recover a third is how a port ends up with
+    // two disagreeing definitions of forward.
+    float   bodyofs[3];         // head in PLAY space (fwd, right, up), world units
+    float   body_yaw;           // play-space -> world yaw, degrees, EXCLUDING the head yaw
+    float   baseorg[3];         // WRITTEN BY THE ENGINE: the view origin BEFORE any VR offset,
+                                // written in the same block as `vieworg`. The pose-residual
+                                // instrument subtracts the two to recover what the camera
+                                // actually did. NOT for the viewmodel mount: CL_AddEntities
+                                // runs BEFORE that block, so a reader there gets last frame's.
 } q2_vr_state_t;
 
 extern q2_vr_state_t q2vr;
